WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.154
Foreign function interfaces are one of the
most common reasons unsafe Rust exists.

2
00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:07.574
Rust may call into C libraries,
operating-system APIs, device interfaces,

3
00:00:07.654 --> 00:00:09.847
embedded platforms, or other external
runtimes.

4
00:00:09.927 --> 00:00:13.633
At those boundaries, Rust's type system
cannot automatically verify every promise

5
00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:14.704
made by the other side.

6
00:00:14.784 --> 00:00:16.402
Review should start with ownership.

7
00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:18.570
Who owns the memory passed across the
boundary?

8
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:19.433
Who allocates it?

9
00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:20.087
Who frees it?

10
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.942
Is the same allocator used on both sides?

11
00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:24.736
Can the external code retain a pointer
after the call returns?

12
00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:27.583
Can Rust safely use the value after
handing it to external code?

13
00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:30.951
These questions determine whether the
boundary is safe to use repeatedly.

14
00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.745
Error conventions and data representation
also need review.

15
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:37.166
External code may signal errors through
return values, output parameters,

16
00:00:37.246 --> 00:00:39.490
global state, or platform-specific
conventions.

17
00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.128
Strings and buffers may have different
encoding, length,

18
00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.617
and termination expectations.

19
00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:47.247
Structures may rely on layout guarantees
that must be documented and preserved.

20
00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:49.414
Threading and callbacks add more
assumptions.

21
00:00:49.494 --> 00:00:51.165
External code may not be thread-safe.

22
00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:54.063
It may call back into Rust after the
original owner has gone away.

23
00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:56.962
It may require a callback to remain valid
for a certain lifetime.

24
00:00:57.042 --> 00:00:59.861
These assumptions need explicit design,
not hopeful comments.

25
00:00:59.941 --> 00:01:03.594
A good FFI wrapper converts external
behavior into safe Rust types and clear APIs as

26
00:01:03.674 --> 00:01:04.509
soon as practical.

27
00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:08.243
Callers should not have to remember every
pointer, lifetime, and allocation rule.

28
00:01:08.323 --> 00:01:11.403
The wrapper should capture those rules,
enforce them where possible,

29
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:14.980
and document the remaining safety contract
where enforcement is not possible.
